# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Until this file existed the list lived in the settings page and nothing in
# the repository could be compared against it, so a job could be added to
# ci.yml, report on every pull request, and never gate a merge — which is
# exactly what happened. Branch protection required only
#
#     ["test-ubuntu-latest","validate mkfs_erofs (fsck.erofs strict)"]
#
# while the last main run produced three green checks. #118 is that gap.
#
# Discovery is not the answer either. It requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is
# a guess at the PR gate and drifts silently: a renamed job, an added
# workflow, or any future check reporting under the github-actions app changes
# what is required without anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and
# makes the intent reviewable in a diff.
#
# WHY `test-ubuntu-24.04-arm` IS HERE, AND WHY IT IS NOT A LUXURY.
#
# It is this repository's only aarch64 coverage, and it runs the full suite
# including the `--ignored` oracle opt-in, so it is the only place a
# target-dependent defect can be observed at all. The failures it sees and
# `test-ubuntu-latest` cannot are the ones that depend on the target rather
# than on the code: `c_char` is `u8` on aarch64-linux and `i8` on x86_64, so a
# cast through it is correct on one and a clippy error on the other (#89);
# alignment and pointer-width assumptions differ the same way. `rust-fs-ntfs`
# added this exact leg and it immediately found that class of failure
# (rust-fs-ntfs#268). Advisory, it could report red on every pull request and
# merge anyway — which is a check nobody reads.
#
# WHY THE NAMES LOOK DIFFERENT FROM rust-fs-core'S.
#
# The `test` job sets `name: test-${{ matrix.os }}` explicitly, so GitHub
# reports `test-ubuntu-latest`, NOT the `test / ubuntu-latest` spelling a
# matrix job without a `name:` produces (rust-fs-core declares that form). The
# names below are copied from what the API actually reported for main
# 0c16b34, not read off the YAML — a required context that never reports is
# a permanent block on merging, so this is not a place to infer a spelling.
#
# Everything declared here runs on `pull_request` (ci.yml's `on:` block
# carries `pull_request: branches: [main]`). release.yml is deliberately
# absent: it triggers on a version tag, so requiring any check of its would
# block every pull request forever.
#
# Third-party reviewers need no mention: github-protect-main.sh filters
# `select(.app.slug=="github-actions")` on every query it makes, so nothing
# reporting under another app can enter the required set through this file or
# through discovery.
#
# The better end state is one always-run aggregate job that `needs:` all three
# and fails on failure/cancelled/skipped — then the matrix can grow a leg
# without anyone editing this file. That is not in this change: with three
# checks, all of which have been green on main, declaring the real names is
# the smaller and more reviewable step.
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
